that has got to be horrific.

00:09:56.257 --> 00:09:58.147
That's just got to be torturous.

00:09:58.147 --> 00:10:01.577
But when you take a
little bit of hate venom,

00:10:01.577 --> 00:10:02.865
and a little bit of hate venom,

00:10:02.865 --> 00:10:05.122
then you develop an immunity to it.

00:10:05.122 --> 00:10:05.975
You kind of understand what it is.

00:10:05.975 --> 00:10:07.164
- [Host] It becomes enjoyable!

00:10:07.164 --> 00:10:09.798
- Well, then it becomes
a checking mechanism,

00:10:09.798 --> 00:10:12.251
and I look at it almost like
as if it's an algorithm.

00:10:12.251 --> 00:10:16.037
And I look at like, well,
this negative comin' in,

00:10:16.037 --> 00:10:18.944
like, how much does it affect me,

00:10:18.944 --> 00:10:23.111
and how much does it affect
the positivity that I put out?

00:10:24.129 --> 00:10:26.975
Like, and if I dwell on it,

00:10:26.975 --> 00:10:28.841
well then it'll surely (beep) me up,

00:10:28.841 --> 00:10:29.858
but if I just look at it

00:10:29.858 --> 00:10:32.417
and if I truly look at myself to say,

00:10:32.417 --> 00:10:35.268
okay, do they have a point?

00:10:35.268 --> 00:10:36.208
And if they do have a point,

00:10:36.208 --> 00:10:37.260
if I am bein' a (beep),

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.498
like, I got to, that's very important

00:10:39.498 --> 00:10:41.916
to be honest about that
and to look at that.

00:10:41.916 --> 00:10:43.668
I enjoy a lot of things,

00:10:43.668 --> 00:10:45.874
and what I've found that the best way

00:10:45.874 --> 00:10:47.452
to live my life for me.

00:10:47.452 --> 00:10:49.138
I'm not sayin', you know, other people,

00:10:49.138 --> 00:10:50.866
some people really love their life

00:10:50.866 --> 00:10:52.484
to sit around and f--k off.

00:10:52.484 --> 00:10:53.479
When I sit around a f--k off,

00:10:53.479 --> 00:10:54.754
I don't like me.

00:10:54.754 --> 00:10:56.001
I like to get things done.

00:10:56.001 --> 00:10:57.120
But the things that I do,

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:58.965
they're recreational, they're all fun.

00:10:58.965 --> 00:11:01.670
Like, Disneyland was fun
as f--k with my kids,

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:03.557
the Eddie Bravo Invitational
was fun as f--k,

00:11:03.557 --> 00:11:04.844
the UFC was fun as f--k,

00:11:04.844 --> 00:11:06.832
my show, sold-out show at the KA Theater

00:11:06.832 --> 00:11:09.373
where they do the Cirque de Soleil at MGM

00:11:09.373 --> 00:11:11.318
with Joey Diaz and Jim Norton,

00:11:11.318 --> 00:11:12.727
Friday night, fun as f--k.

00:11:12.727 --> 00:11:14.360
- [Host] The book is fun as f--k, too.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.252
- The book, those are jobs, right?

00:11:16.252 --> 00:11:17.781
But those are fun things.

00:11:17.781 --> 00:11:19.448
And training is fun,

00:11:20.582 --> 00:11:22.683
stand-up comedy couldn't be any funner.

00:11:22.683 --> 00:11:24.562
You know, the only real work work

00:11:24.562 --> 00:11:26.081
is like, I got to discipline myself

00:11:26.081 --> 00:11:27.874
to sometimes work out
when I don't want to,

00:11:27.874 --> 00:11:29.423
write when I don't want to.

00:11:29.423 --> 00:11:31.340
But this is how I feel,

00:11:32.993 --> 00:11:35.918
it's harder for me to not
do what I'm supposed to do

00:11:35.918 --> 00:11:37.692
than it is for me to do
what I'm supposed to do,

00:11:37.692 --> 00:11:39.210
'cause if I don't do
what I'm supposed to do,

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:41.794
then I deal with regret and doubt.

00:11:41.794 --> 00:11:42.961
I've done it before.

00:11:42.961 --> 00:11:44.712
I've done it a lot of my life,

00:11:44.712 --> 00:11:45.849
I've felt those feelings,

00:11:45.849 --> 00:11:46.891
I don't like it.

00:11:46.891 --> 00:11:49.500
So what I do is I just don't do that.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.273
I just say, well, what is the difference?

00:11:51.273 --> 00:11:52.110
If I don't do it,

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:53.585
then I'm going to have to
feel like I didn't do it,

00:11:53.585 --> 00:11:54.676
and feel like a loser,

00:11:54.676 --> 00:11:56.270
so get after it, b---h.

00:11:56.270 --> 00:11:58.879
We were talkin' before this podcast

00:11:58.879 --> 00:12:01.518
about Holly Holm's
victory over Ronda Rousey,

00:12:01.518 --> 00:12:03.130
and one of the things
that we were talkin' about

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:06.419
was that Ronda had this very linear,

00:12:06.419 --> 00:12:07.823
straightforward attack.

00:12:07.823 --> 00:12:09.011
You knew she was coming,

00:12:09.011 --> 00:12:12.011
and Holly is a master at countering,

00:12:12.887 --> 00:12:16.196
so all she had to do was wait and move,

00:12:16.196 --> 00:12:18.256
and Ronda was coming in one direction.

00:12:18.256 --> 00:12:21.025
There was no variation--

00:12:21.025 --> 00:12:22.993
- There was no creativity.
- There was no creativity.

00:12:22.993 --> 00:12:26.051
It was a mad bulldog rush that had worked

00:12:26.051 --> 00:12:27.927
on everybody else before.

00:12:27.927 --> 00:12:32.440
But she found one person who
was a virtuoso at movement,

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:35.439
and she needed creativity
and it wasn't there,

00:12:35.439 --> 00:12:38.004
and she needed that
experience that came with

00:12:38.004 --> 00:12:41.001
having faced someone
who knew that position

00:12:41.001 --> 00:12:44.171
and had a deep understanding
of that movement,

00:12:44.171 --> 00:12:46.270
and she didn't have
that in her repertoire,

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:48.306
and so that's the result that we saw.

00:12:48.306 --> 00:12:50.222
Like a striker like Anderson Silva

00:12:50.222 --> 00:12:52.206
is extremely creative.

00:12:52.206 --> 00:12:56.013
If you watch, he's got a
fight versus Tony Fryklund

00:12:56.013 --> 00:12:57.547
in Cage Warriors.

00:12:57.547 --> 00:12:58.380
Cage Warriors?

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:00.271
What the f--k was it called, in England?

00:13:00.271 --> 00:13:01.923
Small organization in England.

00:13:01.923 --> 00:13:02.756
I think it's called Cage Warriors--

00:13:02.756 --> 00:13:03.627
- [Host] It was Cage Warriors, yeah.

00:13:03.627 --> 00:13:08.388
- Where he practiced this
step-in uppercut elbow,

00:13:08.388 --> 00:13:09.898
like a sideways elbow,

00:13:09.898 --> 00:13:10.823
and his coach was goin',

00:13:10.823 --> 00:13:13.424
"You're f--kin' crazy,
stop practicing that,"

00:13:13.424 --> 00:13:15.790
and he would make his
wife hold the pillow,

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:17.715
because his coach didn't want him

00:13:17.715 --> 00:13:18.816
to practice it anymore,

00:13:18.816 --> 00:13:20.312
'cause he thought he was wasting his time.

00:13:20.312 --> 00:13:23.265
So he practiced, his wife
would hold a pillow for him,

00:13:23.265 --> 00:13:26.120
and he'd step in and
throw this uppercut elbow.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:27.970
That's what he knocked out Fryklund with.

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:31.040
And obviously Fryklund was
outclassed in that fight,

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.066
but he wanted to make a point.

00:13:33.066 --> 00:13:34.506
And like the front kick that he landed

00:13:34.506 --> 00:13:36.053
in the face of Vitor Belfort.

00:13:36.053 --> 00:13:37.690
Vitor never saw that shit comin',

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:39.056
'cause nobody had done that to him before,

00:13:39.056 --> 00:13:41.355
'cause nobody had done that
in the history of the UFC.

00:13:41.355 --> 00:13:42.924
Nobody had ever knocked anybody out

00:13:42.924 --> 00:13:45.958
with the first kick you
learn in martial arts.

00:13:45.958 --> 00:13:48.902
But the creativity to
try something like that,

00:13:48.902 --> 00:13:52.485
he would throw punches to
your thigh from standing,

00:13:52.485 --> 00:13:53.963
he would throw a jab to your thigh.

00:13:53.963 --> 00:13:55.747
He would throw a crescent kick,

00:13:55.747 --> 00:13:57.573
an inside crescent kick to your face,

00:13:57.573 --> 00:13:58.730
like what the f--k?

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:02.000
It was part of what made him
such an effective striker,

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.225
is that he threw these
things that you just

00:14:04.225 --> 00:14:06.100
didn't expect him to do.

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:07.801
People who are in jiu jitsu

00:14:07.801 --> 00:14:09.023
and train on a regular basis,

00:14:09.023 --> 00:14:10.514
they're healthier people.

00:14:10.514 --> 00:14:12.037
Their egos are healthier, especially men.

00:14:12.037 --> 00:14:13.385
They're easier to talk to,

00:14:13.385 --> 00:14:14.621
they're easier to hang out with

00:14:14.621 --> 00:14:17.901
because they're facing
reality on a regular basis.

00:14:17.901 --> 00:14:18.734
If somebody,

00:14:18.734 --> 00:14:23.474
my (mumbling) teacher told
me when I was a little kid,

00:14:23.474 --> 00:14:26.084
that I never forgot was that martial arts

00:14:26.084 --> 00:14:29.764
are a vehicle for developing
your human potential,

00:14:29.764 --> 00:14:31.876
and nothing in my life has ever

00:14:31.876 --> 00:14:35.864
put me in face with reality
better than jiu jitsu,

00:14:35.864 --> 00:14:39.221
'cause I think, you
know, in life we can all

00:14:39.221 --> 00:14:41.325
distort our perception of things

00:14:41.325 --> 00:14:43.291
in order to make ourselves
more comfortable,

00:14:43.291 --> 00:14:45.696
in order to make ourselves
accept where we are,

00:14:45.696 --> 00:14:46.911
and there's a lot of people out there

00:14:46.911 --> 00:14:49.147
that're runnin' around
in life full of s--t.

00:14:49.147 --> 00:14:51.475
You can't be full of
s--t and do jiu jitsu.

00:14:51.475 --> 00:14:52.890
When you do jiu jitsu it's impossible

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:55.849
to be full of s--t because
reality comes at you

00:14:55.849 --> 00:14:57.540
in the purest form possible.

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.120
A life-or-death struggle
using your determination,

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.103
your focus, your techniques, your mind,

00:15:02.103 --> 00:15:04.018
and your training, over
and over and over again.

00:15:04.018 --> 00:15:04.974
And it's reality,

00:15:04.974 --> 00:15:06.916
and if you f--k up and you
get caught in a triangle,

00:15:06.916 --> 00:15:09.201
you've got to tap and that
is the end of the story.

00:15:09.201 --> 00:15:11.854
It's as real as it can get,

00:15:11.854 --> 00:15:14.704
and that has made me a better person.

00:15:14.704 --> 00:15:16.375
It has made me a better man,

00:15:16.375 --> 00:15:17.763
it's made me understand myself,

00:15:17.763 --> 00:15:19.533
my weaknesses, my strengths,

00:15:19.533 --> 00:15:21.093
the s--t I need to work on,

00:15:21.093 --> 00:15:24.309
jiu jitsu has been one of
the most valuable tools

00:15:24.309 --> 00:15:26.093
I've ever had in my life.

00:15:26.093 --> 00:15:28.760
(kicks echoing)

00:15:38.786 --> 00:15:41.453
(blows echoing)

00:15:42.495 --> 00:15:44.912
(vocalizing)

